module examples.least

// The program the smallest host runs. One function the host provides, one the
// host calls, and the least else. The name is the one the command line provides
// as well, so `kest run` answers what the host beside it answers: what it is for is that `examples/least.c` can
// be read from top to bottom in one sitting, which is what somebody writing
// their first host needs. Everything else this boundary can do is in
// `examples/embed.kest` beside the host that reads it. See D624.
extern fn Host.write(value: text)

fn greeting(who: text) -> text {
    return "hello, {who}\n"
}

// An answer the language has no text of its own for, which is where a host
// with its own structs begins: the machine writes one the way a program writes
// one since D876, and `examples/embed.c` is the host that lays one out its own
// way instead. See D628.
struct Pair {
    a: i32
    b: i32
}

fn pair() -> Pair {
    return Pair(1, 2)
}

// And the shape that still has none, which is what a handle is: what a store
// says as text is what is behind it, and reaching through one is the host's
// question rather than the language's. A host asking for this one as words is
// told which type it was rather than being handed minus one in silence.
fn held() -> store<Pair> {
    let it: store<Pair> = store()
    add(it, Pair(1, 2))
    return it
}

// What the smallest host calls when it is asked for something other than
// `main`: an answer that is not a number, which a host reads the same way it
// reads one. See D628.
fn motto() -> text {
    return "a host reads what came back the same way, whatever it is"
}

// A run of calls that comes back round, which is the other thing a host writing
// its first one has to meet: a program with one of these has no worst chain to
// add up, so `kest_needs` has no answer and the host has to say how many frames
// it allows. What a frame costs is the widest body, so the slots follow from
// the frames — and `examples/least.c` reads both numbers back out of two
// machines to show it. See D815.
fn depth(n: i32) -> i32 {
    if n <= 0 {
        return 0
    }
    return depth(n - 1) + 1
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    Host.write(greeting("host"))
    return depth(3) - 3
}
